a new warning about a rare inflammatory  syndrome tied to the corona virus in  children more than 330 cases have been  reported and at least four children have  died and now doctors say it could also  affect young adults CBS News senior  medical correspondent dr. Tara Narula  joins us Tara good morning to begin with  what our doctors learning about this new  development well Anthony by and large we  are still calling this syndrome a  disease or syndrome that affects  children and teenagers in fact the case  definition by the CDC requires that an  individual be less than the age of 21  now that's not to say that we may not  see sporadic cases or emerging cases in  individuals that are over 21 that's  really a cut-off that's a little bit  arbitrary in the sense that we know that  our biology and physiology doesn't  change tremendously between 21 23 and 24  so as we see some of these cases that  fit the other clinical manifestations  emerging in a little bit of an older  population the case definition of may  change I think it just fits with what  we're seeing with this virus is that  we're learning more and more each day  and really what this does is alert adult  cardiologists like myself an internist  to be aware if they see somebody with  some of these symptoms and signs to be  on high alert you may want to refer them  and and you may want to learn your  health department remind folks what the  symptoms are tara so primary symptoms  are going to be fever moderate or high  fever that lasts for several days  abdominal symptoms and children so  nausea vomiting diarrhea a rash  bloodshot eyes and then worsening  symptoms might be things like chest pain  or shortness of breath or lethargy or  confusion all of those should prompt  parents to pick up the phone call their  pediatrician and potentially take them  to an emergency room if necessary  ideally one that has a pediatric  intensive care unit  all right Tara dr. Tara Narula thank you  very much
 

